      "S7"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Dieter Zetsche is a German engineer and former chief executive of Daimler. He joined the company’s research department after studying electrical engineering, then held management posts in Brazil, Argentina and the United States. After leading Mercedes-Benz, he became chairman of tourism group TUI’s supervisory board and chaired African Parks Foundation Germany.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Selling Chrysler forced Dieter Zetsche to separate a business decision from his attachment to the company. He had spent five years in Detroit leading the American manufacturer and regarded them as among his happiest. As head of its German parent, however, he concluded that the expected benefits of combining the businesses had reached their limit.\n\nThe sale went ahead before financing conditions deteriorated during the credit crisis. Zetsche later used the experience to explain why leaders must decide promptly without waiting for endless analysis. He also recalled facing analysts when Chrysler’s earlier recovery faltered, explaining the losses and the measures he intended to take.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 94,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "University account of his own leadership lecture supports both the personal conflict and specific management lessons; opinions remain attributed."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S8"
    ],
    "word_count": 104,
    "html": "<p>Selling Chrysler forced Dieter Zetsche to separate a business decision from his attachment to the company. He had spent five years in Detroit leading the American manufacturer and regarded them as among his happiest. As head of its German parent, however, he concluded that the expected benefits of combining the businesses had reached their limit.</p>\r\n<p>The sale went ahead before financing conditions deteriorated during the credit crisis. Zetsche later used the experience to explain why leaders must decide promptly without waiting for endless analysis. He also recalled facing analysts when Chrysler’s earlier recovery faltered, explaining the losses and the measures he intended to take.</p>\r\n"
  },
